CAT = "s24Offerings"; SEG = "OFF"
Q = [
('Under Section 5 of the Securities Act, before a registration statement is filed, an underwriter generally may not:',
 'Make offers to sell the security', ['Hold internal syndicate planning talks', 'Publish routine research on other issuers', 'Perform due diligence on the issuer'],
 'Section 5(c) prohibits offers to sell or buy before a registration statement is filed, subject to safe harbors; Section 5(a) prohibits sales until it is effective. Preliminary negotiations among underwriters and the issuer are allowed.'),
("During the waiting period, a firm may publish a notice limited to basic facts such as the issuer's name, the security, the price range and the underwriters under:",
 'Rule 134', ['Rule 144', 'Rule 10b-5', 'Rule 15c3-1'],
 'Rule 134 permits limited communications, often called tombstone ads, that are not deemed a prospectus, once a registration statement has been filed.'),
('A free writing prospectus used in a registered offering after the registration statement is filed must generally:',
 'Carry a legend saying where to get the prospectus', ['Be reviewed and approved by the SEC before use', 'Be shorter than one printed page', 'Omit any information about price'],
 'Rule 433 requires a free writing prospectus to bear a prescribed legend stating that a registration statement and prospectus are filed and how to obtain them, and in many cases to be filed with the SEC.'),
('SEC Rule 163B lets any issuer, or a person acting for it, test the waters before or after filing a registration statement with:',
 'QIBs and institutional accredited investors', ['Retail investors who sign a written waiver', "Any person who reads the issuer's website", "Only the issuer's current employees"],
 'Rule 163B, adopted in 2019, extends test-the-waters communications to all issuers, but only with potential investors reasonably believed to be QIBs or institutional accredited investors.'),
('Under FINRA Rule 5110, the underwriting terms of a public offering that must be filed with FINRA are due no later than:',
 'Three business days after the SEC filing', ['The day the offering is priced', '30 days after the offering closes', 'Ten business days before the registration becomes effective'],
 'Rule 5110 requires the documents and information for a filed public offering to be submitted to FINRA no later than three business days after filing with the SEC, and FINRA must state it has no objection before the offering proceeds.'),
('Unregistered securities that an underwriter receives as compensation in an IPO are generally subject under Rule 5110 to a lock-up of:',
 '180 days from the start of sales', ['30 days from the effective date', '90 days from the pricing date', "One year from the offering's closing"],
 'Rule 5110(e)(1) generally bars sale, transfer or hedging of unregistered securities received as underwriting compensation for 180 days beginning on the date of commencement of sales of the public offering.'),
('Under FINRA Rule 5121, a member has a conflict of interest in an offering if it will receive at least what share of the net offering proceeds, not counting underwriting compensation?',
 '5%', ['1%', '10%', '25%'],
 'Rule 5121(f)(5) defines a conflict of interest to include the member and its affiliates receiving 5 percent or more of the net offering proceeds, as well as control of the issuer, which includes beneficial ownership of 10 percent or more of its common equity.'),
('A member that has a conflict of interest in a public offering under Rule 5121 must generally:',
 'Disclose it prominently, usually with a qualified independent underwriter', ['Withdraw from the offering entirely and refund the issuer', 'Sell the shares only to institutional investors', "Get each buyer's written consent to the conflict of interest"],
 "Rule 5121 requires prominent disclosure of the conflict and, unless an exception applies (such as a lead manager without a conflict who meets the rule's conditions), participation of a qualified independent underwriter in due diligence."),
('A member sells a private placement covered by FINRA Rule 5123. It must file the offering document with FINRA within:',
 '15 calendar days after the first sale', ['3 business days after filing with the SEC', '30 days before the first sale', 'One year after the offering closes'],
 'Rule 5123 requires the member to file the private placement memorandum, term sheet or other offering document with FINRA within 15 calendar days of the date of first sale, or to say none was used.'),
('Under FINRA Rule 5122, when a member privately offers its own securities, the offering document must disclose that at least what share of the proceeds will be used for business purposes?',
 '85%', ['50%', '70%', '95%'],
 'Rule 5122 requires that at least 85 percent of the offering proceeds be used for business purposes described in the private placement memorandum, which must be filed with FINRA at or before the first time it is given to an investor.'),
('Under Rule 506(d) of Regulation D, the exemption is lost if a covered person, such as a paid placement agent, has:',
 'A disqualifying event, such as a recent fraud conviction', ['Sold another private offering earlier that year', 'Fewer than 35 accredited investors in the deal', 'A customer complaint that was denied'],
 'Rule 506(d), the bad actor rule, disqualifies an offering if the issuer or a covered person, including compensated solicitors and their principals, has a listed disqualifying event, such as certain convictions, orders or bars.'),
('An officer of a public company wants to sell shares he bought in the open market years ago. Because he is an affiliate, under Rule 144 his sales are:',
 'Subject to volume limits, with no holding period', ['Free of any condition, since he bought them publicly', 'Barred until he leaves the company', 'Allowed only through a registered offering'],
 'Shares an affiliate bought in the market are control securities, not restricted securities. They have no holding period, but resales under Rule 144 must meet its volume, manner of sale, current information and notice conditions.'),
('Rule 144A provides a safe harbor for resales of restricted securities to:',
 'Qualified institutional buyers', ['Any accredited individual investor', "Retail investors in the issuer's home state", 'Employees of the issuer'],
 'Rule 144A lets holders resell restricted securities to qualified institutional buyers, generally institutions that own and invest on a discretionary basis at least $100 million in securities of unaffiliated issuers.'),
('Under Regulation M Rule 101, for a security with an ADTV value of at least $100,000 and an issuer public float of at least $25 million, the restricted period begins:',
 'One business day before pricing', ['Five business days before pricing', 'The day the registration statement is filed', 'Ten business days after the offering closes'],
 'Rule 100 of Regulation M sets a one-business-day restricted period for securities with ADTV of $100,000 or more and a public float of $25 million or more, and five business days for all other securities.'),
("Under Regulation M, distribution participants may keep trading an issuer's securities that have an ADTV value of at least $1 million and a public float of at least $150 million because they are:",
 'Actively traded securities', ['Exempt securities', 'Restricted control securities', 'Penny stocks'],
 'Rule 101 excepts actively-traded securities, defined as those with an ADTV value of at least $1 million issued by an issuer with a public float of at least $150 million, unless the issuer or an affiliate is the distribution participant.'),
('Under Regulation M Rule 104, a stabilizing bid in a public offering:',
 'May not be above the offering price', ['May be at any price the manager chooses', 'Must be at least 5% above the offering price', 'May be made only after the offering closes'],
 'Rule 104 permits stabilizing bids only to prevent or retard a decline, generally at or below the offering price, with notice to the market and disclosure in the prospectus.'),
('An investor sold short a stock four business days before a follow-on offering was priced. Under Regulation M Rule 105, the investor:',
 'Generally may not buy shares in that offering', ['Must cover the short through shares in the offering', 'May buy shares if they are paid for in cash', 'May buy shares but only from another dealer'],
 'Rule 105 bars buying in a firm commitment offering if the person sold short the security during the restricted period, generally the five business days before pricing, subject to limited exceptions.'),
('An underwriter defending a Section 11 claim over a misstatement in the registration statement can use the due diligence defense by showing it:',
 'Investigated reasonably and believed the statements', ["Relied only on the issuer's written word", 'Was not the lead manager of the deal', 'Sold fewer shares than the other underwriters'],
 'Under Section 11(b)(3), an underwriter avoids liability for unexpertised portions if, after reasonable investigation, it had reasonable grounds to believe and did believe the statements were true and complete.'),
('Under SEC Rule 14e-1, a tender offer must stay open for at least:',
 '20 business days from when it starts', ['5 business days from when it starts', '30 calendar days from the filing date', '60 calendar days from the filing date'],
 'Rule 14e-1(a) requires a tender offer to be held open for at least 20 business days, and Rule 14e-1(b) requires at least 10 business days after an increase or decrease in the percentage sought or the consideration offered.'),
('During a cash tender offer, Rule 14e-5 generally bars the dealer-manager from:',
 "Buying the target's shares outside the offer", ['Giving holders a copy of the offer document', "Answering holders' questions about the offer", 'Being paid a fee by the offeror for its work'],
 'Rule 14e-5 prohibits covered persons, including the offeror and its dealer-manager, from purchasing or arranging to purchase the subject securities except under the tender offer, from announcement until it expires.'),
('Since 2024, a person who acquires beneficial ownership of more than 5% of a class of registered equity securities with control intent must file Schedule 13D within:',
 'Five business days', ['Two business days', 'Ten calendar days', '45 days after the calendar year ends'],
 "The SEC's 2023 amendments to Regulation 13D-G, effective February 5, 2024, shortened the initial Schedule 13D filing deadline from 10 calendar days to five business days after crossing 5 percent."),
('A reporting company must generally file a Form 8-K to report a material event, such as entry into a material agreement, within:',
 'Four business days of the event', ['24 hours of the event', '15 calendar days of the event', 'Its next quarterly report on Form 10-Q'],
 'Form 8-K generally must be filed within four business days after the occurrence of the reportable event.'),
('In a corporate liquidation under the absolute priority rule, which class is paid first?',
 'Secured creditors, from their collateral', ['Preferred stockholders, at liquidation value', 'Subordinated debenture holders', 'Common stockholders, pro rata'],
 "Under the Bankruptcy Code's priority scheme, secured claims are satisfied from their collateral first, then priority and general unsecured claims, subordinated debt, preferred stock and finally common stock."),
("A clause in a loan agreement that bars the borrower from taking on more debt without the lender's consent is:",
 'A negative covenant', ['An affirmative covenant', 'A call provision', 'A sinking fund provision'],
 'Negative covenants restrict actions such as new borrowing, asset sales or dividends. Affirmative covenants require actions such as delivering financial statements or keeping insurance.'),
]
